Sure! To pull out every key‑value pair that comes **after** the `userId` key in your `dataAnalysisContext` object, you can use the `getNextKeyValues` helper.  
Below is a quick example of how you’d call it. If you already have the JSON for `dataAnalysisContext`, just replace the placeholder string with that JSON.

```javascript
// Example JSON for dataAnalysisContext (replace with your real data)
const dataAnalysisContext = {
  userId: 42,
  name: "Jane Doe",
  age: 29,
  email: "jane.doe@example.com",
  address: {
    street: "123 Main St",
    city: "Anytown"
  },
  preferences: ["email", "sms"]
};

// Convert the object to a JSON string (the tool expects a string)
const ctxString = JSON.stringify(dataAnalysisContext);

// Call the helper function
const result = getNextKeyValues({
  ctx: ctxString,
  currentKey: "userId"
});

console.log(result);
```

### What the function returns
`result` will be an array of objects, each containing a `key` and its corresponding `value`, for every pair that appears **after** `userId` until a new nested object or array is encountered. For the example above, you’d get:

```json
[
  { "key": "name",   "value": "Jane Doe" },
  { "key": "age",    "value": 29 },
  { "key": "email",  "value": "jane.doe@example.com" }
]
```

> **Note:**  
> - The function stops collecting pairs when it hits a nested object (`address`) or array (`preferences`).  
> - If you want to include nested structures as well, you’ll need to adjust the logic accordingly.

